1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,220
This video is a general guide to your first assignment, the E D A mini project that you present around week seven.

2
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:11,464
Always follow your own assignment brief for the exact requirements.

3
00:00:11,464 --> 00:00:17,544
This guide shows how to structure your work, using what you have learned in modules one and two.

4
00:00:18,344 --> 00:00:23,090
The assignment is a formative mini project worth twenty five percent.

5
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:25,223
It covers outcomes one and two:

6
00:00:25,223 --> 00:00:34,854
analysing the statistical and structural characteristics of a dataset, and applying univariate, bivariate and multivariate E D A techniques.

7
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:39,944
You present your findings, so both the analysis and the explanation count.

8
00:00:40,744 --> 00:00:42,848
Follow a clear structure.

9
00:00:42,848 --> 00:00:45,458
Start with a problem statement.

10
00:00:45,458 --> 00:00:46,973
Then a data audit.

11
00:00:46,973 --> 00:00:54,128
Then univariate analysis, bivariate analysis, and where suitable a multivariate step.

12
00:00:54,128 --> 00:00:55,475
Then hypotheses.

13
00:00:55,475 --> 00:00:59,011
And finally a story with a recommendation.

14
00:00:59,011 --> 00:01:03,304
Each stage maps to a lecture you have already seen.

15
00:01:04,104 --> 00:01:06,186
Choose your dataset carefully.

16
00:01:06,186 --> 00:01:14,097
Pick a dataset with enough rows, a mix of numeric and categorical columns, and a question that matters to someone.

17
00:01:14,097 --> 00:01:20,343
Check that you may use the data, and that it is not just a toy example everyone else uses.

18
00:01:20,343 --> 00:01:21,939
Write the one sentence:

19
00:01:21,939 --> 00:01:27,768
this sample can support claims about, blank, and cannot support claims about, blank.

20
00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:32,224
In the analysis, pair every statistic with a plot.

21
00:01:32,224 --> 00:01:36,611
For skewed columns, report median and I Q R beside the mean.

22
00:01:36,611 --> 00:01:42,680
Check outliers with the one point five I Q R rule and state what you did with them.

23
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:47,725
Use correlation with a scatter plot, and cross-tabs with percentages.

24
00:01:47,725 --> 00:01:53,648
Remember that correlation is not causation, so label your findings as hypotheses.

25
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:56,691
Make the presentation a story.

26
00:01:56,691 --> 00:01:57,289
Context:

27
00:01:57,289 --> 00:02:01,475
what situation is the data about and why does it matter?

28
00:02:01,475 --> 00:02:02,148
Conflict:

29
00:02:02,148 --> 00:02:06,110
what did the data reveal that is surprising or wrong?

30
00:02:06,110 --> 00:02:06,932
Resolution:

31
00:02:06,932 --> 00:02:08,577
what do you recommend?

32
00:02:08,577 --> 00:02:17,248
Use one chart per message, an insight title on each slide, an honest axis, and highlight the one thing that matters.

33
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:20,996
Use this checklist before you submit.

34
00:02:20,996 --> 00:02:22,748
Is the problem stated?

35
00:02:22,748 --> 00:02:27,209
Are duplicates, missing values and hidden zeros handled?

36
00:02:27,209 --> 00:02:30,237
Do charts have honest axes and labels?

37
00:02:30,237 --> 00:02:32,786
Are skew and outliers explained?

38
00:02:32,786 --> 00:02:35,654
Are hypotheses labelled exploratory?

39
00:02:35,654 --> 00:02:38,761
Does every slide have an insight title?

40
00:02:38,761 --> 00:02:42,744
And can you explain every number if you are asked?

41
00:02:43,544 --> 00:02:45,344
Common mistakes to avoid:

42
00:02:45,344 --> 00:03:00,392
deleting outliers silently, reporting the mean of a skewed column, trusting one plot, treating zeros or nine ninety nine as real values, using truncated bar charts, and making claims the sample cannot support.

43
00:03:00,392 --> 00:03:02,624
Good luck with your assignment.

44
00:03:02,624 --> 00:03:04,064
Thanks for watching.

